{"turn":5,"portal":true,"elapsedMs":55844,"answer":"UNIVERSAL ONE\nWhen I said “CUDA-Q access,” I meant software running quantum simulations on a conventional CPU—not an active GPU service or a physical quantum computer.\n\nCUDA-Q is a software framework, not a particular machine. The important distinction is what runs the calculation:\n\n- CPU simulator—our current CUDA-Q connection. An ordinary computer calculates how a quantum system is expected to behave. There are no physical quantum bits in that calculation.\n- GPU simulator. A conventional processor designed for parallel calculations can accelerate suitable simulations. It is still simulating quantum physics, not performing a physical quantum experiment. Paid GPU access is not enabled here.\n- Actual quantum hardware. A device manipulates and measures real quantum systems. Our separate hardware route is QuEra Aquila through Amazon Braket, which uses neutral atoms. Launching a run requires a reviewed plan and explicit confirmation; live device availability has not been checked.\n\nFor a biologist, the distinction resembles simulating an experiment on a computer versus performing an experiment at the bench. Both can be useful, but they provide different evidence.\n\nThe Atlas records earlier GPU simulations and physical Aquila runs, followed by separate local CPU checks. Those historical runs are distinct from the connection available now. [1][2]\n\nOne biological limit matters: our recorded atom responses are not measurements of proteins binding DNA. Establishing useful binding predictions requires comparison with laboratory binding measurements. [1][2]\n\nYour login changes funded-study permissions, not the type of processor. No new calculation has been launched in this exchange.\n2 sources"}
